WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.280
Once upon a time, there was a boy who was&nbsp;
born into a family of simple tailors in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.620
middle of sub-Saharan Africa. The boy’s father&nbsp;
wanted him to continue the family business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:16.440
but the boy wanted a better life and one night&nbsp;
decided to leave his home and go to Europe.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.800
To cross the sea the boy built himself&nbsp;
a raft. But once he left the shore,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.140
there was only a little wind and it didn’t&nbsp;
take long for his supplies to run out. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:33.480
there he was: hungry, and lonely, floating&nbsp;
in the middle of the mediterranean sea.

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.700
Imagine you live on the coast of&nbsp;
Southern Europe and one morning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.400
read about the boy's fate in a local&nbsp;
paper. Should you yourself try to save him?

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:56.100
Four influential philosophers — Aristotle, Mill,&nbsp;
Kant, and Nietzsche — can help us answer this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.000
and other difficult moral questions&nbsp;
with their respective theories.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:08.040
Virtue theorists like Aristotle ask:&nbsp;
how to live a good life? The best life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:13.920
according to him, is the life of someone who&nbsp;
demonstrates excellence in aspects of a virtuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:19.800
character: the capacities for emotion,&nbsp;
theoretical ideas, and practical wisdom.

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:27.900
Aristotle would argue that if helping the boy is&nbsp;
a manifestation of a personality that’s virtuous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:31.740
then you should save him because&nbsp;
being a person of good character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:36.720
is what matters. What that exactly&nbsp;
means, differs for every one of us.

00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:45.180
If you are rich and powerful, saving the boy could&nbsp;
be easy. It might still be the right thing to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:50.460
but it doesn’t reveal much of your personality,&nbsp;
and hence isn’t particularly virtuous.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:56.700
If you have little to no means,&nbsp;
your decision to save the boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.040
is much harder. If you still try to do&nbsp;
so, then this truly reveals character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.400
is virtuous, and is something&nbsp;
you probably should try doing.

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:14.700
Utilitarians such as John Stuart Mill, ask which&nbsp;
action increases well-being for most people?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:20.820
So when faced with a choice they do what&nbsp;
seems most beneficial to most people as if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.840
they could calculate what consequences&nbsp;
lead to the highest net happiness.

00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:32.400
When thinking about saving the boy's life, you&nbsp;
should consider all the happiness the boy and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:38.820
his family get, the joy it may bring you, and&nbsp;
the benefits for society now and for generations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:45.720
to come. This you then compare with what else you&nbsp;
could be doing — known as your opportunity costs.

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.300
For example, instead of risking your life at sea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.080
you could work the day in a coffee&nbsp;
shop, and donate the money you made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:59.700
to an effective charity which then saves&nbsp;
two kids from starvation somewhere else.

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:07.140
Comparing the two options, you decide&nbsp;
not to save the boy, but instead work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:14.640
donate and double the total potential well-being&nbsp;
of humanity. Some call this effective altruism.

00:03:17.100 --> 00:03:22.020
Deontologists like Immanuel Kant,&nbsp;
focus on the 'intent' rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:28.140
consequences. They believe in universal&nbsp;
moral laws, such as “Don't lie. Don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:33.720
steal. Don't cheat.” The golden rule is&nbsp;
the so-called categorical imperative:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:39.240
our decisions are then morally right when they&nbsp;
can become a rule everyone else should follow.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:44.520
If you help the boy, because&nbsp;
it makes you look good,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.520 --> 00:03:49.440
your intentions are wrong because you&nbsp;
treat him as a means to an end and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:54.180
don’t want to live in a world in which&nbsp;
false heroism is a universal moral norm.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:04:00.900
If you save the boy, because you want to&nbsp;
live in a world in which helping those in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:06.420
need is always the right thing, then you&nbsp;
should do that. You should even do so if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:10.800
you have reason to believe that the kid may&nbsp;
end up taking advantage of your kindness.

00:04:13.080 --> 00:04:19.320
Friedrich Nietzsche would ask what’s in your&nbsp;
own interest? He argued that self-interested&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:24.120
behavior is morally right as it makes&nbsp;
us stronger. And if we are stronger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:30.540
so is society. Acting against your own interest&nbsp;
is immoral because it hinders this development.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.540
So if you want to help the boy,&nbsp;
because you think that is good for you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.280
do it. Help the boy. But if you&nbsp;
think saving the boy could hurt you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.160
don’t. It’s not your responsibility to save&nbsp;
those who are too weak to help themselves.

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:56.940
So what do you think? Did any of the four help you&nbsp;
with that decision? If not, here is one more idea.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:04.440
Scholar Moshe Koppel made the interesting&nbsp;
observation that Utilitarians and Kantians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:10.620
don’t use their own moral principles when trying&nbsp;
to falsify each other’s theories. Both rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:16.980
appeal to a feeling — as if we all, inside us,&nbsp;
actually know what’s right and what’s wrong.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.420
Kantians would say: you don’t want to live&nbsp;
in a world in which children die in the open,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:31.620
because you decided to save two strangers instead.&nbsp;
Such behavior just doesn't feel right. Correct?

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:38.100
Utilitarians would counter: surely&nbsp;
you wouldn’t want to save, say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:42.180
a psychopath from drowning who’d then&nbsp;
go ahead to kill you and your family,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:48.420
just because helping people is always right&nbsp;
by principle? That just feels wrong. Right?

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:57.720
So now tell us, would you save the boy? And why?&nbsp;
Is your decision based on the character you strive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:04.440
to become, an analysis of costs and benefits,&nbsp;
behavior we want to see in the world, for your own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:10.980
self-interest, or does it maybe just feel right?
